PrismOS: take libdrm's Linux code paths.

xf86drm.c finds and classifies DRM nodes through /sys/dev/char/<maj>:<min>/...
(drmNodeIsDRM, drmGetMinorNameForFD, the subsystem and uevent parsing of
drmGetDevices2); the kernel serves those paths (sys/kern/src/fs/sysfs.rs, the
display is a platform device).  Without this, an unknown OS falls into the BSD
branches -- <sys/ioccom.h>, devname_r, ... -- that mlibc does not have.

xf86drm.h and include/drm/drm.h (installed as libdrm/drm.h) are public
headers: their ioctl macros and types are the Linux ones too (drm_handle_t is
an unsigned int there, an unsigned long in the BSD branch -- an ABI mismatch
with the kernel's structs), and every client (xorg-server's modesetting
driver) sees them.

__prismos__ is what the gcc patch of ports/toolchain defines.
drmCheckModesettingSupported (xf86drmMode.c) is left alone: it reads
/sys/bus/pci, which is not served.

diff -ru a/include/drm/drm.h b/include/drm/drm.h
--- a/include/drm/drm.h	2024-12-04 19:30:30.000000000 +0100
+++ b/include/drm/drm.h	2026-11-27 07:08:26.931609003 +0100
@@ -35,7 +35,7 @@
 #ifndef _DRM_H_
 #define _DRM_H_
 
-#if   defined(__linux__)
+#if defined(__linux__) || defined(__prismos__)
 
 #include <linux/types.h>
 #include <asm/ioctl.h>
